{"text": "6112\n\nActivation of Keratinocyte Nicotinic Cholinergic\n\nReceptors Stimulates Cal:\nCell Differentiation\n\nHuman epidermal keratinocytes synthesize, secrete,\nand degrade acetylcholine and use thelr eell-aurface\nnicotinic and muscarinic cholinergic receptors to\nmediate the autocrine and paracrine effects of acetyl-\ncholine, Because acetylcholine modulates transmemt-\nbrane Ca\" transport and intracellular metabolism in\nseveral types of cells, we hypothesized that cholin-\nexgic agents might have similar effects on ke\ncytes, Nicotine increased in a concentration-depen-\nnner the amount of Cs\" taken up by\nytes isolated from human neonatal fore~\nskins. This effect was abolished in the presence of the\nspecific nicotinic antagonist mecamylamine, indicat-\nlng that it was mediated by keratinocyte nicotinic\nacetylcholine receptor(s). The sequences encoding\nthe \u2018a3 and a7 nicotinic receptor subunits were amn~\nplied from cDNA isolated from cultured keratino-\ncytes. These subunits, as well as the a3, 2, and Bt\nsubunits previously found in keratinocytes, can be\ncomponents of Ca\u2122-permeable nicotinic receptor\nchannels. To learn how activation of keratinocyte\n\ncium Influx and Enhances\n\nnicotinic receptors affected the rate of cell differen\nation, we measured the nicotinie cholinergic effects\non the expression of differentiation markers by eul-\ntured Keratinocytes. Long-term incubations with\nmicromolar concentrations of nict\ncreased the number of cells forn\nJopes and the number of cells\nto suprabasal keratin 10,\ninvolucrin, and filaggrin. The increased production\nof these differentiation-associated proteins was veri-\n\nfied by Western blotting. Because nicotinic cholin-\ncergic stimulation causes transmembrane Ga trans\nport into keratinocytes, and because changes\n\nin concentrations of intracellular Ca?* are known to\nalter various keratinocyte functions, includin,\n1\u00a2 subcellular mechanisms medi\nthe autocrine and paracrine actions of epidermal\nacetylcholine on keratinocytes may involve Ca*\nsecond nger.\n\nGrando, S.A., Horton, R.M., Mauro, T.M., Kist, D.A., Lee, T.X., and Dahl, M.V.\n\nJournal of Investigative Dermatology 107:412-418, 1996.\n\nOther support: National Institutes of Health,\n\nFrom the Departments of Dermatology of\n\n\u2018University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, MN,\n\nand University of California, San Francisco, CA.\n\nPUBLICATIONS\n\n>\n\nyh\n\n0052433\n\n60044273",
